  "declined": false,
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
      "quote": "An examiner will need to consider whether the presence of an INN stem in a word is “meaningful” enough to give rise to a connotation leading to likely deception or confusion under section 43. Some INN stems consist of simple two or three letter combinations and their presence in a larger word may not be meaningful and as a result would not lead to deception or confusion."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
      "quote": "In addition, a stem may not be included meaningfully in a trade mark where its presence is clearly overwhelmed by the meaning of the trade mark as a whole."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/2",
      "quote": "Stems can be prefixes, suffixes or infixes - this is denoted by the position and presence of hyphens in the list of INN stems."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/5",
      "quote": "A ground for rejection under section 43 exists where a trade mark to be used in relation to pharmaceuticals or veterinary substances consists of an INN stem, or contains a notified INN stem in a meaningful way, and use of the trade mark in respect of the goods covered by the specification are not restricted to substances indicated by the INN stem."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/5",
      "quote": "A section 43 ground for rejection may be overcome by the applicant agreeing to a condition of registration limiting use of the trade mark to goods containing substances belonging to the relevant INN stem."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part22/24/24/2",
      "quote": "A ground for rejection under section 41 is appropriate where a trade mark to be used in relation to pharmaceutical or veterinary substances, is in its entirety identical to a notified INN stem. Such a trade mark is not considered capable of distinguishing the applicant’s goods and a ground for rejection would be very difficult to overcome."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
      "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Mere overlap with letters on the INN stem list does not automatically establish a ground for rejection. The next step is to assess whether the stem is included meaningfully; short letter combinations may be incidental. [TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1]\n\n**Manual practice (not legislation):**\n1. Check the stem’s designated position—prefix, suffix or infix—and assess its meaning in the trade mark as a whole. The whole mark’s meaning may overwhelm the stem’s significance. I cannot determine meaningful inclusion from the letter overlap alone. [TMM/Part29/5/5/2; TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1]\n2. For pharmaceuticals or veterinary substances, the Manual states that a section 43 ground exists where the mark consists of an INN stem, or meaningfully contains a notified INN stem, and use for the specified goods is not restricted to substances indicated by that stem. Meaningful inclusion and the goods/use restriction therefore both matter. [TMM/Part29/5/5/5]\n3. That section 43 ground may be overcome by an agreed condition of registration limiting use to goods containing substances belonging to the relevant stem. This is a possible response, not an automatic outcome. [TMM/Part29/5/5/5]\n4. Separately, if the entire mark is identical to a notified INN stem for pharmaceutical or veterinary substances, the Manual identifies a section 41 ground based on lack of capacity to distinguish, which it says would be very difficult to overcome. This is distinct from merely containing matching letters. [TMM/Part22/24/24/2]\n\n**Act:** Section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the mark, or a sign within it, makes use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion. It does not make appearance on the stem list itself the statutory test. [TMA1995/s43]"
}
